ay  but think again  because all interns salary is actually  in a very similar range of mediocre so  when you graduate with a  two hundred thousand dollar debt versus  a ten thousand dollar debt you're  actually significantly reducing your  income paying a thousand dollars is very  different from five hundred dollars when  you're just starting out luckily i  figured this out before i started my  masters so two of my top criteria was  cost and the speed at which i could get  my degree  because one or two extra years that  makes a huge difference in your debt  and the potential income you could be  having by working so i went back to  ryerson which is where i went for my be  arc because they gave me an awesome  scholarship and they had a fast track  program which let me graduate and one  and a half years compared to two to  three or even three and a half years  from some other programs throughout my  master's i work part-time as a teaching  assistant if you can try your best to  get a job at the architecture school  because it's very convenient and they  pay quite well thanks to my smart moves  i graduated with only  50 000  in student loans but it wasn't until i  graduated that the gravity of my loans  really started to kick in and that year  i probably entered one of the lowest  points in my life fifty thousand dollars  was just a massive amount for me and i  felt constantly overwhelmed with the  idea of having to pay it back especially  on my tiny tiny tiny intern salary it  didn't help that all my friends were  working while i was in school they were  all making more money than me i'm never  going to forget that one day when i told  them how much money i made  they all  were shocked because you know they  thought that i have a bachelor's and a  master's and they thought that i would  be making way more money  and this put me in an even worse  downward spiral i had no motivation i  wasn't enjoying my work and i was  becoming resentful of everyone and of  myself let me just pause for a quick  moment for a message from our sponsor hp  as an architect i spend a lot of time  reading the fine print on the  environmental impact of some of the  products and materials i'm specifying i  try to look at the material content and  where it comes from but also the  company's manufacturing process or the  recycling program hp is a company that's  actually making a lot of strides on  their sustainability agenda not just  using it as a marketing pitch so their  newest initiative is a partnership with  trees for jane which is an international  grassroots campaign with the goal of  stopping deforestation and helping  communities around the world and so all  of your hp purchases from now through  june is going to contribute to planting  1 million trees if you've never heard of  dr jane goodall she's the founder of the  jane goodall institute and she's also a  un messenger of peace  and the foundation's been doing some  really amazing things including their  goal to plant one million trees by the  end of 2022. hp's products and their  packaging contain recycled material like  their hp envy 7955e  which is made with 45 recycled plastic  their products also contain ocean bound  plastics  they also have a recycling program where  you can send back your empty cartridges  and they'll recycle it for you so you  know it's going to the right places  anyways all of your hp purchases from  now through june is going to contribute  to this initiative so  if you need to buy any printers or  products this is probably a good time to  buy so anyways back to my story there  was one thing that really helped me out  of this downward spiral and that was  making a plan so first i sat down and  analyzed my debt  and i set an ideal payoff date for me  that ideal timeline was two years  because that was the time that i set for  myself to get my architect's license so  it was actually kind of easy for me to  visualize myself in this new life newly  licensed architect with no debt then i  used an online calculator like this one  where you can adjust the time span and  it's going to tell you how much you need  to pay each month to pay it off by that  day from this i got my number i then  scheduled in my diary to check in with  myself every week to make sure that i  was on track once i set that goal  it became very obvious that i had to do  two things  i had to seriously increase my income  and i had to seriously reduce my  spending i started packing my lunch i  stopped eating out i cut my inessential  spending but there's one thing that made  the biggest dent in my spending moving  back to my parents house this was  actually a really tough decision and a  huge blow to my ego because i've been  living that independent woman life for  the past six years and i wasn't about to  move back to my mom's house but once i  set up my plan this was kind of a  no-brainer for me so the strategy here  is to ignore your parents for six years  and when you come back they're gonna be  so happy they're gonna be showering you  with love and food  joking now i know that i'm very very  fortunate to have this but where you  live is a major major contributor to  your income and your cost of living and  if i didn't have this i might have  actually moved to another city where the  cost of living was not so high i would  have definitely gotten a roommate the  second thing i was looking for was any  areas where i could increase my income  so the first thing that i did was  actually ask for a raise and  that didn't go very well  and so the next thing that i did was  looking for any freelance services i  could provide on the side the problem is  when you're an intern you don't have  that much experience but there are some  things that you are going to be good at  so this is when i sat down and made a  list of all my strengths to try to  figure out what my marketable strengths  were  after an exhaustive list i came up with  two skills rendering and photography so  i told everyone i knew if you know  anyone who needs renderings or  photography services i'm the one i can  do it cheap and well so i started  getting some rendering jobs here and  there i was starting to meet my monthly  financial goals but i was really working  around the clock because you know big  surprise i was charging too little for  my services till one day  all of that changed  [Music]  i have been working for a client who  owned a pastry franchise called stuffies  i actually met this client through my  boss at the time a quick tip here your  firm and your bosses are actually gonna  be your biggest untapped resource for  finding your side income when you're an  architect and especially when you have  your own firm  you get a lot of people asking you to do  work that's just too small and not  feasible for the firm to do like  renderings in my case  this was my boss's old client from the  days when he was an intern and  freelancing i took this on i did the  renderings but i also did any kind of  graphics or promotional banners and kind  of anything and everything and over time  i gained their trust uh so one day this  uh stuffy's client asked me if i could  do a design for their new store at the  eden center in toronto because they're a  franchise they already have all of their  design guidelines that talks about the  color font signage feel atmosphere so my  job was to just adopt it for the new  space and you know figure out the  circulation and make it functional it's  not the most interesting work for a  designer but once you do it once it's  relatively easy to do another and  another and another of course for  construction drawings you can charge a  lot more than just doing renderings in  bc for projects of a certain type up to  a certain size you actually don't need a  licensed architect as long as you have  another licensed professional like an  electrical or a mechanical engineer and  actually  this is like when you're an intern  is the perfect time for you to be doing  the small project because once you  become a licensed architect no matter  how small the project you need to sign  off on the drawings and you can't really  do that without a liability insurance or  a certificate of practice  which actually cost thousands of dollars  once you become a licensed architect  you're actually a lot more limited to  what you can and you can't do i have a  video where i talk about the pros and  cons of getting licensed i'll leave it  right here so anyways this kept  happening i would get hired to do one  thing like renderings or photography and  i get asked to do the design or  construction drawings as a young  graduate you might think that you don't  have the skills to make side income but  you actually do if you just keep your  ears open and tell everyone you know  that you're providing these services  and now you have things like fiverr or  upwork right just get into the habit of  showing off your work and your skills  and once you get the job  meet deadlines do amazing work be  responsive be nice and pleasant to work  with do such a good job  that when they need other design  services they're gonna be like oh wait  um can you do it  so this is a very quick breakdown of how  i paid off my student loans in two years  yes i was very fortunate to be able to  live rent free at my mom's and i was  lucky to work for bosses who would  regularly throw work my way but none of  this would have manifested in my life if  i hadn't made a concrete step-by-step  plan to pay off my loans of course i had  months where things didn't go as planned  but when you don't have a roadmap you're  just letting your loans control you when  you set a goal and track that goal and  do whatever it takes to meet that goal  you will start to see gaps and  opportunities that you hadn't seen  before  if you're an intern architect one of the  fastest ways you can increase your  salary is to get licensed and get more  letters after your name if you're  interested in learning about how i got  licensed in less than four years of  working i'll leave the video right here  if you're not interested in that and you  just want to see some cool architecture  videos i have a video on my  architectural thesis i'll leave it right  here if you want to see more content  like this hit the subscribe button and  you'll get more content on career and  design and creativity if you want to get  notified next time i release a new video  you can hit the notification bell with  that i'll see you guys in the next video  [Music]  you

